WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.267
Filtering is how we turn a business question into a subset of rows.

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:10.697
In this example, the first question is: which web orders have an amount of at least one hundred?

00:00:10.697 --> 00:00:16.707
We create two conditions and combine them using the ampersand operator, which means AND.

00:00:16.707 --> 00:00:19.707
Notice the parentheses around each condition.

00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:24.857
In Pandas, those parentheses are important when combining Boolean expressions.

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:31.442
The second example uses query to find all orders with amount greater than or equal to one hundred.

00:00:31.442 --> 00:00:34.597
Click Run and compare the two results.

00:00:34.597 --> 00:00:37.952
Filtering should always connect to a meaningful question.

00:00:37.952 --> 00:00:42.602
Instead of thinking, I need to practice greater-than syntax, think:

00:00:42.602 --> 00:00:45.887
which transactions exceed our review threshold?

00:00:45.887 --> 00:00:48.907
Which patients are older than sixty-five?

00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:51.922
Which products had sales below target?

00:00:51.922 --> 00:00:58.297
That change in thinking matters because the syntax becomes attached to a real analytical purpose.

00:00:58.297 --> 00:01:02.222
Also remember that a filter changes which rows are included.

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:06.831
Before continuing, it is often useful to check how many rows remain.
